values
  ('Leadership & Workplace Culture', '', '13 Years of Leadership Lessons from ''World''s Best Boss''', 'When my team gifted me a coffee mug with ''World''s Best Boss'' written on it for my 38th birthday, I reflected on how long it had taken me to earn that…', 'When my team gifted me a coffee mug with ''World''s Best Boss'' written on it for my 38th birthday, I reflected on how long it had taken me to earn that title — 13 years, 11 months, and 15 days.
From a clueless 24 year old who was given a team to manage on Day 1 of his work life to a 38 year old leadership coach who is deeply respected by his team, life has come a long way.
Here''s what I learnt along the way and if it makes sense, feel free to try:
• Keep it real - about yourself, about the org and about life. Your team not only can take it but prefers it over a rosy picture.
• Model the way. Your team does what you do, not what you say or ask them to do.
• Care genuinely. Not because it gets the job done but because it''s your responsibility as a leader; genuinely is the key word, people see through sooner or later.
• Be humble. Your position today is a result of some of your hard work and lots of luck (birth lottery, nurture and contributions of people around you); the position will be forgotten, the person will be remembered.
• Stay emotionally regulated. You might not win something big with this but will certainly avoid big losses. Practice the pause and get back with a response, not a reaction.
• Smile and have fun (most important). Create a workplace your team looks forward to coming to rather than getting anxious on Sunday nights.', 0),
  ('Career Paths & Professional Development', 'Economic Times', 'Why Most L&D Programmes Fail Before They Even Begin', 'Despite best intentions and increasing budgets, most Learning & Development (L&D) programs struggle to create sustained impact.', 'Despite best intentions and increasing budgets, most Learning & Development (L&D) programs struggle to create sustained impact.
Why? Because many organizations jump straight into training for skills, skipping the very factors that determine whether the learning will stick at all.
Think about what L&D typically looks like: an LMS stacked with modules on time management, feedback, or communication or a workshop with a facilitator guiding a team through soft skills exercises. It''s structured, organized… and often ineffective.
That''s because skill is not the beginning of the learning journey - it''s the final step. To move from training to true transformation, we need to look at what happens before someone is ready to acquire a skill. We need to understand the inner world of the learner.
And this led me to develop a simple yet effective learning framework: WISE - a four-part model that includes Will (Intent), Intensity (Energy), Synergy (Alignment), and Expertise (Skill). Let''s explore each layer.
Will (Intent) — The Inner Spark: Learning starts with intent. Adult learners are not like schoolchildren. They''re not motivated by scores or grades - they are driven by internal motivators like growth, mastery, and meaning. Yet many corporate L&D programs ignore this completely.
Intensity (Energy) — The Inner Fuel: Intent is powerful but it needs fuel. That fuel is energy - not just physical, but mental focus, emotional connection, and spiritual alignment. If someone is burned out, overwhelmed, or disengaged, no amount of skill training will yield results.
Synergy (Alignment) — The Inner Fit: Even with intent and energy, people need to be in the right role doing the right kind of work. When there''s synergy between personal strengths and organizational needs, people take initiative, ask better questions, and apply what they learn.
Expertise (Skill) — The Outer Expression: Only now - with Will, Intensity, and Synergy in place, is it time to focus on Expertise. Skill development becomes meaningful only when it''s layered on top of internal readiness.
If we want L&D to create real, lasting change - in behavior, performance, and culture - we must shift the focus from what we teach to who we''re teaching. L&D isn''t just about building skills, it''s about building people - WISEly.', 1),
  ('Career Paths & Professional Development', 'People Matters', 'Not Everyone Should Be a Leader, and That''s a Good Thing', 'For over a decade, the corporate world has echoed one feel-good mantra: "Everyone is a leader." You''ve seen it on onboarding decks, leadership…', 'For over a decade, the corporate world has echoed one feel-good mantra: "Everyone is a leader." You''ve seen it on onboarding decks, leadership programs, and LinkedIn posts. It sounds inclusive, empowering and, frankly, misleading. Let''s get honest. Leadership isn''t for everyone.
Not because people aren''t capable but because true leadership demands something deeper than competence. It demands character, courage and clarity. The willingness to take the harder path when it''s easier to conform. And that''s a path many aren''t prepared for, and that''s okay.
The myth of skill-based leadership: Modern leadership development has reduced leadership to a toolkit of observable behaviours - candour, empathy, strategic thinking, and presence. What we''re often teaching is how to act like a leader, not how to become one. You can''t fake conviction, and you can''t simulate inner clarity.
The forgotten discipline: Self-leadership. It''s about who you are when no one is watching. Do your actions match your values? Can you stay centred in complexity? Are you able to watch your ego rise and not let it rule you?
Enter Dharma: the inner compass of real leadership. In Indian thought, Dharma isn''t just duty — it''s the right alignment. Knowing your role and showing up fully in it. Choosing the harder right over the easier wrong. Saying no when it''s easier to say yes and yes when it''s uncomfortable to do so.
"Leadership without Dharma is performance. Leadership with Dharma is presence."
If we continue to push the idea that everyone is a leader, we dilute the weight of what leadership actually is. Let''s stop handing out crowns. Let''s start honouring the discipline of leadership for what it truly is: not a universal birthright, but a conscious commitment - to self, to truth, and to the people we choose to serve.', 2),
  ('Personal Growth & Mindset', '', 'Forget Self-Love — Self-Awareness Is the Real Superpower', 'You don''t need self-love, you need self-awareness and accountability. Let me elaborate how self-love is killing your growth.', 'You don''t need self-love, you need self-awareness and accountability. Let me elaborate how self-love is killing your growth.
Ask about the demerits of the Modi government to a bhakt (Modi lover); only if you are lucky, you might get to know one or two. How does this change when you ask about the areas of improvement to someone who loves themselves? It doesn''t.
Imagine a girl who weighs 20 kgs more than her ideal body weight and feels embarrassed while hanging out with her peer group. She has two choices for self-talk: "It''s okay. My feelings are valid; I am good this way and I love myself" - even shutting down a concerned friend who points it out; or "I am good at A, B and C but my health needs attention. I need to lose weight and improve my overall health. I can do it and I will do it."
This is the difference between Self-love and Self-awareness.
So why is this fad of self-love doing the rounds on all possible social media platforms? Because telling people to put in effort and being accountable is hard - most people don''t receive it well, and those who do, won''t sign up for an expensive course or come back to the next therapy session.
We don''t need Self-Love, we need Self-Awareness. And this is true even for people who are too self-critical. Self-love is delusional, Self-awareness is growth.', 3),
  ('Personal Growth & Mindset', '', 'Vipassana — The Trek of the Mind', 'I spent 10 days without speaking, without my phone, without books, without music - just sitting with my mind. And it changed the way I look at life.', 'I spent 10 days without speaking, without my phone, without books, without music - just sitting with my mind. And it changed the way I look at life.
A trek has two parts: the struggle of climbing the mountain and the joy of reaching the top. Vipassana felt exactly like that.
The Challenges — Routine: Waking up at 4 AM, meditating 10.5 hours a day, simple satvik food, and last meal at 5:30 PM sounds manageable on paper - but adapting to it from a modern lifestyle is hard. My sleep and digestion went for a toss for the first three days, then slowly settled.
Noble Silence: No speaking for 10 days. No phone, no reading, no writing, no music - no stimulus at all. Time almost freezes. I am used to solitude, so it wasn''t unbearable, but it still required adjustment.
Sitting for long hours: Sitting on the floor for nearly 10 hours a day is tough, very tough. With my L4-L5 and L5-S1 issues, the pain felt impossible initially. When I went to the teacher on Day 2, he said: "Pain is part of the process. Observe it. Ask for a chair only if it becomes unbearable." It never became unbearable. I pushed through.
Focusing on the breath: The hardest part. We are so used to distraction that focusing on anything feels difficult. Real focus tires you, and time slows down. I would think 30 minutes had passed only to see it was 10.
The Joy — The Technique: I earlier saw meditation only as a tool for calmness. Vipassana took me beyond that - to purification and equanimity anchored in impermanence. Not impermanence as an idea, but as a direct bodily experience. When you truly feel it, reactions reduce and balance slowly sets in.
The Institution: It is incredible that a 2500-year-old technique taught by Buddha is being offered for the last 57 years purely on voluntary donations without any religious affiliation. You pay nothing unless you wish to donate, but you must follow strict rules: no killing, stealing, lying, sexual misconduct, intoxicants and above all, Noble Silence.
The Man - S.N. Goenka: The evening discourses by Goenka ji were a highlight. Logical, sharp, witty and full of simple examples, he earns respect without demanding devotion.
Bottomline: Is it tough? Yes. Is it worth it? More than you can imagine.', 4),
  ('Leadership & Workplace Culture', 'People Matters', 'Gen Z Isn''t the Problem — You Are', 'GenZ isn''t the problem, you are. Here are some common myths associated with them:', 'GenZ isn''t the problem, you are. Here are some common myths associated with them:
They are rude and entitled - because they are assertive and speak their mind? You have been conditioned not to and hence the assertiveness seems rude.
They lack resilience - because they take leaves on mental health grounds? You also did but called in sick; different words, same action.
They are too sensitive - because they bring their heart to work, hold themselves to high standards and get disappointed when they don''t live up to it? You too were; it''s just that you were forced to learn to not care too much.
They are not loyal and are money-minded - because they demand fair compensation for their work and refuse to get exploited? Because they grew up seeing mass layoffs and know that no org is loyal to their workforce? You also wanted to demand the same but couldn''t so you kept switching your jobs.
They are too choosy about their nature of work - because they want to work on something which they enjoy and seek a purpose bigger than profits? Should it not work this way for everyone? Given a choice, would you not like to do the same even today?
They aren''t hard-working - because they have life beyond work and say no to working on weekends or after office hours? You have been doing that to appease your boss or for your next promotion but do you enjoy doing that?
The expectation that the next generation will behave the way the previous one did is not only unrealistic but detrimental to our progress and if that''s the way it should be, why have you been fighting with your parents asking them to change with time?
Next and last - should both change for you? If you look deep down, you will realise that you just envy them for being able to do things which you wanted to do but couldn''t.
Realise it, accept it and rather than bashing an entire generation from your high horse, get down to learn from them. When that happens, they will also be open to learn from your experience.', 5),
  ('Leadership & Workplace Culture', '', 'The Power of Authenticity in Leadership', 'Why keep it real with your team- the people you lead?', 'Why keep it real with your team- the people you lead?
There are just two ways to lead your people: by telling them the truth, or by letting them find the truth on their own. And both come with their own consequences.
Let''s start with the first one - telling the truth: you earn trust and credibility - not just with your team, but with yourself; helps you sleep better at night. You empower better decisions - even if some of them create short-term discomfort. You receive the truth in return - often uncomfortable but always essential for your growth. You build a culture of integrity - charity begins at home so does culture. You make people feel safe - and safe people show up fully, every day.
And the most important - it''s easy.
How about the second - withholding the truth? You lose trust as people see through it and there''s no damage control. You might win short-term, but you''re building on a shaky foundation. You stay blind to your areas of improvement. You create a culture you never aspired for. You run the most expensive employee engagement activity - the guessing game.
Lincoln said, "You can fool all the people some of the time and some of the people all the time, but you cannot fool all the people all the time."
And that group of people you can''t fool? It''s your team - the people you spend more of your waking hours with at work than with your partner at home.', 6),
  ('Leadership & Workplace Culture', '', 'Your Team Mirrors You — For Better or Worse', 'Why is modeling the way the only way as a leader? Because humans are wired for coherence, not commands.', 'Why is modeling the way the only way as a leader? Because humans are wired for coherence, not commands.
Not convinced? Let me produce some evidence.
Back in 1961, psychologist Albert Bandura conducted a groundbreaking experiment that forever changed how we understand learning and behavior. He showed that children don''t just learn by direct rewards or punishments. Instead, they learn by watching others.
In the famous Bobo Doll experiment, kids watched adults either act aggressively toward an inflatable clown doll or play quietly. The kids who saw aggression were much more likely to imitate that behavior - hitting, kicking, even yelling at the doll while those who didn''t see aggression were calm and gentle.
This experiment highlighted the power of observational learning, showing that what we model as leaders, parents, or colleagues deeply influences how others behave.
Why does this matter for leaders? Because your team tends to do what you do, not what you say. Our actions speak louder and teach more than words ever could.
Still not convinced? Just compare yourself to the manager you''ve been working with for the past two years - you will understand!', 7),
  ('Leadership & Workplace Culture', '', 'Building Trust at Work: The Most Valuable Currency in Leadership', 'Trust is the most important currency at work. As Patrick Lencioni mentions in his book ''Five Dysfunctions of a Team'' - "Teams who lack trust conceal…', 'Trust is the most important currency at work. As Patrick Lencioni mentions in his book ''Five Dysfunctions of a Team'' - "Teams who lack trust conceal weaknesses and mistakes, hesitate to ask for help, jump to conclusions about the intentions of others, hold grudges and dread meetings."
So, how do we build trust? The first thing to keep in mind is that it''s a journey, not an event and every journey takes time and patience. In my experience of working with multiple functions across different industries, the following things work the best:
1. Before people trust, they need to know each other. Be genuinely interested in knowing people deeply and allow them to know you; this involves moving from questions like "what are you watching on Netflix these days?" to "what makes you really happy?"
2. The best way to find out if you can trust somebody is to trust them. And it''s not the best way - it''s the only way which is paved with a mix of satisfaction and regret but the satisfaction obtained from a few outweighs the regret from many. Rather than saying "Trust me," start with "I trust you."
3. Be appreciative, empathetic and consistent. "Appreciation is the key which opens the lock for constructive criticism" as it shows that you are a person who appreciates when something goes right and is ready to give constructive feedback when it doesn''t.
4. Be vulnerable. Being vulnerable doesn''t mean crying your heart out before everyone, it''s about not hiding our human side and giving others a chance to relate to you as a person which goes beyond a manager, colleague or direct report.', 8),
  ('Leadership & Workplace Culture', '', 'The Courageous Side of Genuine Care', 'Caring genuinely is NOT just about remembering birthdays. That''s being sweet which is different from being caring. It is also not a shrewd tactic to…', 'Caring genuinely is NOT just about remembering birthdays. That''s being sweet which is different from being caring. It is also not a shrewd tactic to get things done faster. It is not even about trying hard to make your team like you more - most leaders fall prey to this.
It is simply the way humans are supposed to operate.
And hence caring genuinely means giving someone tough feedback knowing it might make you the bad guy for a while; backing them before others but coaching them behind the scenes; pushing them towards a painful but necessary path, because it''s good for them in the long run.
It means seeing them as human beings first; not just as roles, resources, or ratings. It means asking yourself: what does this person need to grow - not just deliver? And then doing it - even if it''s hard, messy, slow and makes you unlikeable in the short term.
That''s care, not the warm and fuzzy kind, but the courageous kind.
And here''s the trick: You can''t fake this. You either care, or you don''t. People know. They always know.
And when people feel that you genuinely care? They give back with trust, effort, and loyalty that no incentive program or annual hike can buy.', 9),
  ('Leadership & Workplace Culture', '', 'Why True Humility Isn''t About Being ''Nice''?', 'Humility is not being nice. It''s not about saying "yes" to everyone or pretending to be modest. That''s insecurity in disguise, not humility.', 'Humility is not being nice. It''s not about saying "yes" to everyone or pretending to be modest. That''s insecurity in disguise, not humility.
Real humility is clarity. Clarity that your current position is a mix of some hard work and a lot of luck: the birth lottery - where, when, and to whom you were born; the nurture ecosystem - family, mentors, teammates, friends; the invisible hands - people who vouched for you, guided you, supported you when no one else did.
Humility is knowing this truth and not letting your title confuse you. Because positions will be forgotten but how you made people feel will echo much longer.
Leadership isn''t about pleasing everyone. It''s about staying grounded and using your seat not to elevate your ego, but to elevate others - by being kind, not just nice.', 10),
  ('Leadership & Workplace Culture', '', 'The Domino Effect of a Small Setback', 'In the last two weeks, I messed up. An ankle injury that''s been bothering me for a while just… wouldn''t get better. So I finally decided to get some…', 'In the last two weeks, I messed up. An ankle injury that''s been bothering me for a while just… wouldn''t get better. So I finally decided to get some examinations done. But nothing conclusive came out of it and that put me in the zone of uncertainty.
No workouts. No workouts → no motivation to wake up early. No early mornings → no reason to sleep on time. No sleep → no focus during the day. And the cycle kept repeating.
One unresolved issue created a cascade and affected all four sources of energy: Physical - no movement, poor sleep. Mental - cluttered mind, scattered attention. Emotional - low mood, frustration. Spiritual - no stillness, no intentionality.
It''s incredible how one "small" thing can throw everything off.
The truth is: you can''t optimize performance without managing energy. And energy isn''t just about productivity hacks - it''s deeply personal, deeply interconnected.
What I learnt: It''s not about perfection but catching the spiral early enough. And taking one small step to break it.', 11),
  ('Leadership & Workplace Culture', '', 'The Quid Pro Quo Illusion', 'What if the worst trade you ever made… is one you don''t even remember making?', 'What if the worst trade you ever made… is one you don''t even remember making?
There are many ways to live a bad life - chasing the wrong goals, ignoring your intuition, staying in comfort zones. But one of the worst can be summed up in three words: Quid. Pro. Quo.
It looks smart in the short term. You give something, you get something. Clean. Calculated. Efficient.
But what you lose in that transaction often slips past unnoticed: a potential relationship; a ripple of long-term value; an opportunity to build trust that compounds quietly over time.
And here''s the paradox: what you lose is not even in your frame of awareness. It''s an unknown unknown. You don''t know what you missed. And worse - you don''t even realise you missed something.
Focusing only on immediate gain might feel like rational behaviour. But the truly rational choice is adding value, investing in people, and thinking in decades, not days.
It''s just that this kind of math doesn''t fit into simple equations. But those who get it, play a different game and they tend to win big, silently.', 12),
  ('Leadership & Workplace Culture', '', 'Why I Show Up Even When I Don''t Feel Like It', 'I don''t like holding daily stand-ups for my team, at least 10% of the days - days when I am simply not feeling up for it. But I miss attending just…', 'I don''t like holding daily stand-ups for my team, at least 10% of the days - days when I am simply not feeling up for it. But I miss attending just 1% of them - when I am out of office or when the sky is falling due to some other burning issue.
So what do I do with the remaining 9%? I get up, dress up and show up. And I tell my team, "Aaj man nahi tha yaar is meeting ka."
But why not skip it altogether? Because if I do that just because I didn''t feel like it, not showing up for meetings for similar reasons will become a norm in my team diluting the objectives.
Then why tell them that I wasn''t feeling up for it? Because if I do that, expressing feelings gets normalised. And as someone who believes that we work with human beings who bring not just thoughts and skills but also emotions to the workplace, I want to encourage it.
Takeaways: you should be disciplined and not let our mood decide our actions. You should create a space for people to express their feelings.', 13),
  ('Leadership & Workplace Culture', '', 'Brutal Honesty or Lack of Empathy?', 'I once had a manager who would say whatever came to his mind hurting teammates, peers, and even superiors in the process.', 'I once had a manager who would say whatever came to his mind hurting teammates, peers, and even superiors in the process.
You know the kind of person who looks at a presentation and says, "This is shit" instead of "The quality can be improved and here''s how."
Whenever someone gathered the courage to suggest he might want to change his approach, the response was predictable: "I don''t know how to beat around the bush. What you get from me is brutal honesty."
For a long time, I believed that and thought he was just someone with a bitter tongue but a good heart. But over time, as I connected the dots across his other behaviors, it became clear that it was a lack of empathy disguised as honesty.
Since then, I''ve seen many people, both personally and professionally, hide behind the same excuse.
If we closely observe, honesty doesn''t have to be brutal. In fact, truly honest feedback/opinions are rooted in the other person''s growth rather than our own unburdening.
Look closely - do you also have brutally honest people in your life?', 14),
  ('Career Paths & Professional Development', '', 'Three Ways People Find Work They Truly Love', 'There are three paths to discover the work you love:', 'There are three paths to discover the work you love:
The Early Callers: You figured out what you liked and were good at early on - maybe as a child or teen. You stuck with it, kept learning, and slowly built a career around it. If this is you - lucky you. Keep growing and fly even higher.
The Late Finders: You didn''t have one clear path. You tried out different things - jobs, projects, side hustles - didn''t give up and slowly found what clicked. Maybe through trial and error, maybe by accident. If this is where you are - hang in there. Keep trying. You might be slower than others, but you''re still moving forward. And when you do find your thing, you''ll bring so much depth to it.
The Lifelong Explorers: You''ve done lots of things. Some felt nice, some didn''t. But nothing ever felt like "this is it." You keep learning, shifting, evolving - but you''re not attached to one label or lane. If this sounds like you - don''t worry. It''s okay to not have one fixed path. Some people are here to explore, connect dots, and enjoy the ride.
I am a ''Late Finder'' - took me just 38 years.', 15),
  ('Personal Growth & Mindset', '', 'When a Person Can''t Find Meaning, They Distract Themselves With Pleasure', '"When a person can''t find a deep sense of meaning, they distract themselves with pleasure." A few days ago, when Shashank Sharma wrote about this…', '"When a person can''t find a deep sense of meaning, they distract themselves with pleasure." A few days ago, when Shashank Sharma wrote about this idea from Viktor Frankl, it stayed with me. I found myself looking at both my past phases and present days through this lens.
What I noticed was that whenever I wasn''t satisfied with one domain of my life, I tried to compensate through something else - more often than not through pursuits like alcohol, validation, or food. So it wasn''t really about the pursuit but a compensation for something I wanted to achieve elsewhere but couldn''t.
Something similar happened in Machiavelli''s life when he was 43. Stripped of his titles and in exile, he writes about visiting brothels and inns of Florence, seeking validation from women far lower in status. He was aware of what was happening and could see the manipulation but couldn''t stop himself from falling prey to it. He could observe but not act on it.
When I started paying attention to this, I noticed that the urge to smoke, drink, or eat something indulgent is much stronger on days that feel unproductive. It''s as if one domain spills into others.
One way I''ve been able to successfully deal with it is by reducing such days as much as possible: days where there''s neither meaningful work nor genuine rest/fun. And on days when that still happens, I try to not get consumed by the feeling and not suppress it either - just observe it without identifying with it and let it pass.
Have you noticed similar patterns in your life?', 16),
  ('Personal Growth & Mindset', '', 'Discipline Isn''t About Overriding How We Feel', 'Purpose beats motivation and discipline beats both purpose and motivation - that''s what we often hear right? But what does it actually mean to be…', 'Purpose beats motivation and discipline beats both purpose and motivation - that''s what we often hear right? But what does it actually mean to be disciplined?
A lot of advice points in one direction: just show up - wake up at 5, do the work and don''t let your mood dictate your actions. On the surface, that makes sense but it never actually worked for me fully. Because when I force myself to act despite resistance, I can see that I''m not really free of it, I''ve just pushed it aside.
Last Friday reinforced this for me: I woke up in a bad mood. No clear reason; just one of those mornings where everything feels slightly off. Naturally, I didn''t feel like going for a walk or doing anything productive. For a moment, I considered just pushing through it.
But instead, I tried something different - I didn''t try to fix it and I didn''t try to ignore it either. I just sat with it without identifying with it, observing myself from a distance. And then I went ahead with my day, still carrying that same mood and somewhere along the way, without me trying to change it, the feeling passed, just disappeared.
Makes me feel that discipline is not about overriding how we feel or suppressing it, it''s about building our capacity to observe our emotions from a distance.
What has worked for you and what hasn''t?', 17),
  ('Personal Growth & Mindset', '', 'How I Want to Exist, Not What I Want to Achieve', 'As I turned a year older, the question wasn''t what I want to achieve but how I want to exist.', 'As I turned a year older, the question wasn''t what I want to achieve but how I want to exist.
Last week, amidst celebrations with close friends and wishes across multiple social media platforms, I found a few quiet moments for reflection during a solo morning walk. The question of how I want to live the rest of my life sat at the centre of that reflection and the only answer that emerged was this: to move towards greater detachment - detachment without renunciation. To do everything, yet not associate myself as the doer, to simply be a witness like a leaf floating on water, completely wet on one side, yet completely dry on the other.
Over the last few years, I have been able to make some progress in this pursuit: showing up fully without being overly attached to outcomes and letting go of things and sometimes people without getting too affected. Engaging in deep reflection and practices like Vipassana have helped immensely but I also recognize that there is endless scope for growth.
Not an ascetic, not a renunciate but a king who is internally free of his kingdom - fully involved, yet fully detached. I found King Janaka: a sage-king who fulfilled all his responsibilities without getting attached to them. One popular story goes: messengers rushed in shouting that Mithila was on fire. But Janaka did not move. He calmly said, "Mithila is burning but nothing of mine is burning." He did not identify with anything external, not even his kingdom.
That''s how I want to live the rest of my life: not chasing a specific goal but focusing on the state of being I operate from.', 18),
  ('Personal Growth & Mindset', '', 'The Real Challenge Today Is Stepping Away From Validation', 'One critical thing that separates us from animals is this: we don''t just live to survive, we also look for meaning.', 'One critical thing that separates us from animals is this: we don''t just live to survive, we also look for meaning.
Earlier, the struggle was mostly around food and survival. If someone wanted a deeper life, they had to move beyond basic needs and instincts. Today, those basics are easier for many of us but a new layer has taken over: Validation - the need for approval, attention, being seen. It may not be entirely new but social media has amplified it to a different level.
So the real challenge now is learning to step away from the constant pull of validation.
A small practice that helps: before you post, share or even say something, pause and ask yourself, "Would I still do this if no one knew I did it?"', 19),
  ('Personal Growth & Mindset', '', 'No, You Aren''t a Bad Judge of People', 'No, you aren''t a bad judge of people, you just don''t pay attention to the right things. This is what I tell my clients when they talk about betrayal…', 'No, you aren''t a bad judge of people, you just don''t pay attention to the right things. This is what I tell my clients when they talk about betrayal in relationships.
They say, "I am there for you." They ask, "Do you need help?" They say, "Oh nice! This looks lovely." They give you hugs when they meet you. They come across sweet, warm, and humble. And sometimes, they even go on to discuss their problems with you which mostly involves bitching about other people around them. After all, what better way to show you that they trust you?
But they don''t. They use this facade to win your trust and then use it to their advantage.
And you fall for the words, despite learning this multiple times as a child: actions speak louder than words. If you don''t get swayed by the sweet talk and focus on actions, you will observe that: they are rarely there when it actually matters; they speak differently about people behind their backs and eventually, about you too; their help comes with subtle arm-twisting, expectations, or withdrawal later; their consistency is missing - warmth when it suits them, distance when it doesn''t.
So are they Machiavellian? Some of them. Or delusional? Some of them. But does that change how you deal with them? Not really. No focus on words, all focus on actions - towards you, and towards others. Because it''s only a matter of time before the same pattern reaches you.
You don''t need a complex framework to understand people, you just have to stay focused on the right thing which comes with building Awareness in every moment.', 20),
  ('Personal Growth & Mindset', '', 'Cut Off All Toxic People — Good Advice, Right?', 'Cut off all toxic people from your life - good advice, right? Not really. Let''s understand why.', 'Cut off all toxic people from your life - good advice, right? Not really. Let''s understand why.
When we are around toxic people who trigger negative emotions in us, our peace gets disturbed and we all know that. But what''s more important is this: we don''t just lose our peace in that moment, we also add fuel to the fire of negative emotions already simmering inside us. And that harms our personal and spiritual growth.
But is it really possible to cut people off completely? Very difficult. Because we don''t fully control our environment. Many toxic people exist within our families, friendships, and workplaces and we can''t always choose to walk away.
So what can we do instead?
Regulate the interaction - not too much, not too little - just enough to keep things functional without any big announcement or guilt.
Build inner armour - work towards a state where it affects us less, if not zero. This comes not just from understanding, but practicing the principle of impermanence through daily reflection and meditation. When that sinks in, emotions become fleeting sensations: something to observe, not react to.', 21),
  ('Personal Growth & Mindset', '', 'It''s Rarely the Situation That Explodes', 'It''s rarely the situation that explodes, it''s what we''re carrying within. Most people blame external situations for how they feel - angry,…', 'It''s rarely the situation that explodes, it''s what we''re carrying within. Most people blame external situations for how they feel - angry, frustrated, irritated. But if you really look closely, external events are just sparks. On their own, they are too small to cause an explosion.
The explosion happens only when there''s already fuel within, which builds up over time. Part of it comes from our nature but a large part of it is shaped daily through how we interpret experiences, what we hold on to, what we replay, what we refuse to release.
The choice is ours: we can keep adding to the fuel or we can slowly start letting it go and that choice changes everything about how we experience the world. Because when there''s less fuel inside, the same sparks lose their power - and there comes a point when they stop triggering anything at all.
So if it feels like everything around you is constantly bothering you, it might be worth asking: Am I carrying too much fuel within me?', 22),
  ('Personal Growth & Mindset', '', 'Adversities Don''t Make Life Difficult, Our Inner State Does', 'In coaching, most engagements begin with a problem that feels urgent - something the client desperately wants to fix. And that makes sense; after…', 'In coaching, most engagements begin with a problem that feels urgent - something the client desperately wants to fix. And that makes sense; after all, we all want relief.
But over time, I''ve noticed something subtle. It''s rarely about solving the problem immediately, it is about helping the person reach a place where they can make a decision without being hijacked by fear and anxiety.
Whether the decision turns out right or wrong - that''s often perspective but what matters more is the state from which it was made. If someone can choose calmly, fully aware of the consequences, something shifts for the future - they begin to trust their own clarity instead of chasing external reassurance.
Osho once said, "Be calm, and life begins to speak."
When I was a child, I used to pray, "Let everything be good." As I grew older, it became, "Give me strength to fight." Now it has quietly changed to, "Give me equanimity so that I don''t get shaken by the challenges." Because if I am calm in the face of challenges, I will be equipped to tackle them effectively.', 23),
  ('Personal Growth & Mindset', '', 'Not Everything Needs to Be Analysed, Sometimes It Just Needs to Be Noticed', 'We live in a world that''s increasingly obsessed with scientific research. And honestly, that''s a good thing because science and data help us…', 'We live in a world that''s increasingly obsessed with scientific research. And honestly, that''s a good thing because science and data help us understand how things work and help us make better decisions.
But somewhere along the way, something else has quietly taken a back seat - Awareness. Science gives us broad explanations about how the world works but it doesn''t always help us understand what''s happening inside us in a particular moment.
Take a simple example: you attend a meeting at work. Nothing dramatic happens. People talk, decisions are made, the meeting ends. But when you walk out, you feel strangely drained. Now if you try to explain that through science, there could be a hundred possible reasons - maybe you didn''t sleep well, maybe it''s fatigue, maybe it''s screen time, nutrition, stress levels, and so on.
But if you pause for a moment and just pay attention, the answer is often much simpler: maybe you felt unheard in the meeting, maybe the conversation had an undercurrent of tension, maybe the discussion was unnecessarily confrontational, or maybe you just realised that the whole meeting could have been an email.
Awareness often helps you notice the most relevant reason much faster than analysis. But very often, the answer is already there, we just have to slow down enough to notice it. Building that kind of awareness takes effort but once it starts growing, it becomes one of the most reliable compasses you have.', 24),
  ('Personal Growth & Mindset', '', 'Most Relationship Conflicts Don''t Start With What Happened', 'They start with the story we attach to what happened. I''ve been noticing something about why relationships become painful. Often it''s not the…', 'They start with the story we attach to what happened. I''ve been noticing something about why relationships become painful. Often it''s not the situation itself, it''s the meaning we attach to it.
Imagine this: a friend visiting your city calls to see if you can meet. You''re genuinely happy to hear from them and you carve out time on a busy weekday evening - plan fixed, 5 pm. At 4 pm, a message comes: "Something came up. A senior colleague is visiting from the US, I''ll have to spend the evening with them." And suddenly the mind starts moving. "Why didn''t they tell me earlier?" "This isn''t the first time." "Maybe they don''t value my time."
When something like this happens, I''ve noticed that we usually respond in one of three ways: Reaction ("What''s the point? You make plans and never follow through."), Absorption (the resentment quietly stays), or Reconciliation - seeing the situation for what it is: this person tends to make plans and not always follow through. So if I''m free, I can meet. If something else matters more, I can choose that instead without assumptions or resentment.
What changed? In the first two cases, it wasn''t just the plan that got cancelled. Somewhere, our self-worth got attached to the plan. And when behaviour from others becomes a commentary on our worth, relationships start carrying a burden they were never meant to carry.
Sometimes clarity in relationships is simply this: seeing people as they are and adapting without turning their actions into a judgement about ourselves.', 25),
  ('Personal Growth & Mindset', '', 'Every Strength You Admire Exists Because of Something Else You Rarely Notice', 'We often look at strengths and growth areas as if they exist separately. They don''t. Most human qualities come in pairs. They co-exist and sustain…', 'We often look at strengths and growth areas as if they exist separately. They don''t. Most human qualities come in pairs. They co-exist and sustain each other. In fact, they demand something from each other.
Fearlessness cannot exist without honesty. To be truly honest, you must be willing to face consequences. Leadership demands responsibility. Empathy demands boundaries. Ambition demands resilience. Creativity demands discipline.
Every strength carries a developmental requirement. If we embrace the strength but avoid what it demands, it begins to distort. Empathy becomes exhaustion. Ambition becomes restlessness. Honesty becomes silence.
Even what we call ''opportunities for growth'' also have origins. Anger often originates in pride. Anxiety often originates in attachment. Control often originates in fear. Resentment often originates in hurt.
If you want to understand a behaviour, look for its source. If you want to strengthen a virtue, build the capacity it requires. Nothing in human nature stands alone. Traits live in relationships just like we do.
The real question is not just "What is my strength?" or "What is my flaw?" It is - what sustains it, and am I willing to grow into that too?', 26),
  ('Personal Growth & Mindset', '', 'Most Decisions Are Actually Made for You — The AWARE Test', 'I''ve been thinking about how often we choose things without really choosing them. So I started using a simple filter for myself. I call it the AWARE…', 'I''ve been thinking about how often we choose things without really choosing them. So I started using a simple filter for myself. I call it the AWARE test. Before making a decision, I ask:
A - Awareness: Am I choosing this consciously, or just reacting?
W - Why: Can I clearly state why I want this — in my own words?
A - Affect: Is this a real emotion or just an impulse?
R - Reality: Does this make sense practically, or only mentally?
E - External Influence: Would I still choose this if nobody knew I chose it?
Let''s look at some decisions: Working out - I may not feel like it, but my why is strong. Eating a brownie - no real reason, but there is a genuine feeling. Both are conscious choices.
The danger zone is different. It''s when you can''t explain why, you don''t really feel anything, and you''re still doing it. That''s usually conditioning. Not all conditioning is bad because some habits make life efficient. But unexamined conditioning quietly runs your life.
Lately I''ve been trying to live by one rule: if I act, it should come from awareness, not autopilot. Because the quality of your life is not decided by your decisions. It''s decided by who is making them - you or your conditioning.', 27),
  ('Personal Growth & Mindset', '', 'Meaningful Conversations Start With Yourself', 'This post is not about Indigo but Happiness - the kind which comes from meaningful relationships. But meaningful relationships don''t appear on their…', 'This post is not about Indigo but Happiness - the kind which comes from meaningful relationships. But meaningful relationships don''t appear on their own, they''re built through meaningful conversations.
And here''s the part we often forget: you can have meaningful conversations with others only if you''re already having them with yourself. When genuine inner dialogue reduces, genuine outer dialogue disappears too.
Look around - most conversations today revolve around Netflix, food, trends, gossip, and endless updates. If meaningful conversations are fading, it''s because reflection is fading first. Are we so consumed by our screens that we no longer have time to look inward? Or worse - have we mistaken endless scrolling, viral quotes, and "relatable reels" for actual reflection?
This is especially dangerous for the young as youth is when we form friendships that stay with us for life. If those relationships are built without depth, we lose something we may never fully recover.
Maybe it''s time to move from "what are you watching on Netflix?" to "what are you looking forward to in life?" - slow thinking, honest reflection, and real conversations because that''s where strong relationships begin and that''s where lifelong happiness is built.', 28),
  ('Personal Growth & Mindset', '', 'Life Makes More Sense at 30,000 Feet Than on the Ground', 'A few days ago, on a flight from Delhi to Bangalore, I looked down at the city lights. From above, they formed a perfect, breathtaking pattern. And I…', 'A few days ago, on a flight from Delhi to Bangalore, I looked down at the city lights. From above, they formed a perfect, breathtaking pattern. And I wondered - do the people switching on these lights even know that they''re part of something this beautiful? Probably not. They''re just living their lives… unaware of the pattern they''re creating.
And that''s exactly how our lives are. In our 20s and early 30s, we''re mostly in our own heads - careers, money, expectations, self-doubt. It''s easy to feel "too small."
But here''s what I''ve learned with time: you only understand the bigger picture later when your experiences mature and your crystallised intelligence helps you connect the dots. Only then you realise: every effort, every failure, every honest choice was a tiny light in a much bigger design.
If you''re in your 20s, here''s the truth I wish someone told me: Life won''t always make sense from where you''re standing. But from a little distance, it always does. Take breaks. Step back. Zoom out. And one day, when you look back, you''ll see it clearly: you were always part of a cosmic pattern, you just couldn''t see it yet.', 29),
  ('Personal Growth & Mindset', '', 'Your Parents Are Your First Shadow', 'Most people spend their 20s trying to escape their boss. The wiser ones start by understanding their parents.', 'Most people spend their 20s trying to escape their boss. The wiser ones start by understanding their parents.
Last week, when I was chatting with my Gen Z nieces and nephew, I told them something I wish someone had told me earlier - "your parents are your first shadow." You absorb their fears, beliefs, and definitions of love long before you build your own. Their voice becomes your inner voice. Their expectations become your self-talk. And often, their pain becomes your pattern.
Growing up isn''t just about moving out or earning your first paycheck, it''s about noticing where ''they'' end and ''you'' begin. For this generation, that awareness matters even more. You''re the first to have both the tools and the courage to break intergenerational cycles - not by blaming, but by becoming conscious.
So if you ever feel misunderstood, unseen, or stuck - don''t fight the shadow. Understand it. Grow through it.', 30),
  ('Personal Growth & Mindset', '', 'Tragedy Doesn''t Change You — This Does', '"Tragedy changes people." We say this as if it''s automatic. As if pain equals growth. It isn''t.', '"Tragedy changes people." We say this as if it''s automatic. As if pain equals growth. It isn''t.
Some people go through hardship and become more grounded. Others become more cynical. Some become wise. Others become bitter. So let''s stop pretending that experience alone transforms people. It''s not the event. It''s the response.
Two people face the same loss. One shuts down. One opens up. Two people get rejected. One becomes defensive. The other becomes discerning. It''s not what happened, it''s how they met it.
We glorify experience as the great teacher. But experience is neutral. It only becomes transformational when you choose to reflect, reframe, and rise.
So no - tragedy doesn''t change you. You change you.', 31),
  ('Personal Growth & Mindset', '', 'Life Isn''t a Copy-Paste Job', 'Even with MBTI''s limitations, it claims that there are 16 distinct personality types in this world. And hence other than the natural law of survival,…', 'Even with MBTI''s limitations, it claims that there are 16 distinct personality types in this world. And hence other than the natural law of survival, isn''t it fair to assume that there will be 16 distinct ways of looking at life and making decisions?
So why is it necessary for everyone to study science and become a doctor/engineer? Get a high paying job in an MNC? Get married and have kids? If it''s not, why is the majority doing this? Because we are conditioned by society to act in a particular way and however rational we think we are, most of our actions are governed by that conditioning, just to feel belonged.
When we were kids, we did as we were told by our parents and elders but can we now ask ''why'' to ourselves - before picking up a job, before getting married, before going for kids?
And even if you don''t come up with a strong reason and tell yourself, "I thought about it. Though I don''t have a strong reason behind doing this, I just feel like doing it," by all means, go ahead and do it; at least, you are well aware of the pros and cons and are making an informed decision.
Do I really love traveling or is it a FOMO induced by people''s pics on Instagram? Do I really like K-pop or is it because people my age listen to it and talk about it? If we all stop thinking and start repeating the same thing, the world will be an echo chamber and living in an echo chamber for long drives people crazy.
"I think therefore I am" - Descartes.', 32),
  ('Personal Growth & Mindset', '', 'The Missing Ingredient Between You and Big Success', 'Do you want to make it big? If the answer is yes, what''s stopping you? There are millions of ''how to'' videos to help you learn new skills and tons of…', 'Do you want to make it big? If the answer is yes, what''s stopping you? There are millions of ''how to'' videos to help you learn new skills and tons of productivity apps to inculcate good habits and help you track your progress. Then why is it not happening?
Because there''s one thing which is missing - in the popular discourse on success and in your life. And that one thing is Intent.
Not convinced? Look around and ask yourself - in your last few interactions, how many of your friends told you that they are aspiring for a Big Hairy Audacious Goal - like becoming the next CEO or building a billion dollar enterprise, let alone working towards it? Hardly any? Maybe 1-2? And what did the rest say? "Bas bhai, chal raha hai."
And that "chal raha hai" or lack of intent is precisely the first blocker in their path to success, if not the only one. Where there is no thought, there is no action. The pursuit of Mastery starts with Intent, not with skill. And most people don''t have it - difficult to admit but true.', 33),
  ('Personal Growth & Mindset', '', 'Six Steps to Pea(k)ce Performance', 'Imagine walking with a glass full of water and colliding with someone, what gets spilled? Water. What if it contains coffee? Coffee. And what if it…', 'Imagine walking with a glass full of water and colliding with someone, what gets spilled? Water. What if it contains coffee? Coffee. And what if it has shit? Shit, right?
Now since we agree that a glass can only spill what it contains, let''s draw a parallel with Life. You are the glass, your thoughts and feelings are the contents of the glass, you colliding with others are the situations and people you encounter in your daily life. And this essentially means that in the face of good, bad and ugly circumstances, your responses will be determined by what''s inside you. So doesn''t it make sense to sort out your contents?
And how to do that? By consciously designing your life for Peace; not for the crests of joy or troughs of sorrow, but for that steady experience - that steady and straight horizontal x-axis. And this can be done easily by: sticking to a morning routine - exercise, sunlight, meditation, cold shower for happy hormones; eating right - same balanced diet everyday; learning (growing) and contributing to the work you love, during the day; following a night routine - having dinner with friends/family for belongingness and reading/journaling for reflection before hitting the bed at a sane time; getting good quality and enough quantity of sleep.
And most importantly, by having an abundance mindset and being hopeful for the future. And this needs to be done everyday because Life is lived every day, not only on weekends or vacations.', 34),
  ('Personal Growth & Mindset', '', 'Be Here Now', '"Are you reading this post?" "Yes." "Are you ''just'' reading this post without doing/thinking anything in parallel?"', '"Are you reading this post?" "Yes." "Are you ''just'' reading this post without doing/thinking anything in parallel?"
If the answer is still a Yes, stick to this practice in whatever you do and you don''t have to read further. But if it changes to a No, we have a problem.
But what problem are we talking about? Isn''t it better to multitask or think about something while doing something else? It is, if you want to eat more resulting into poor health, reduce focus leading to bad decisions, listen passively creating weak connections.
And none of us wants any of the above but find it cumbersome to do something natural - being present.', 35),
  ('Personal Growth & Mindset', '', 'Abundance Mindset Isn''t Positive Thinking', 'Abundance mindset is not Positivity. It''s Security.', 'Abundance mindset is not Positivity. It''s Security.
We hear a lot about abundance. "Think big." "Give freely." "Believe there''s enough for everyone." Sounds great but far from reality. In the last three workshops I have conducted on Happiness - more than half of the participants admitted that they don''t feel that way deep down.
So what is an Abundance Mindset and more importantly, what is it not? It''s not about positivity, it''s about security. It''s not about ignoring problems or repeating affirmations. It''s about truly believing that I am enough, there is enough, and what''s meant for me will not miss me. That inner security creates space to grow, give, and celebrate others.
And how do you build security? Not just by journaling and affirmations. You build it through evidence. Every small win. Every day you show up when it''s hard. Every time you finish what you started. You don''t need a miracle to feel secure. You just need a few small, honest wins and the self-awareness to acknowledge them.
Abundance isn''t a mindset you switch on. It''s a lost feeling you earn back, one quiet win at a time.', 36),
  ('Personal Growth & Mindset', '', 'If You Want to Grow, Be Open', 'What do you do when someone presents a point of view different from yours? You can cut them short, dismiss it completely, and force your own point of…', 'What do you do when someone presents a point of view different from yours? You can cut them short, dismiss it completely, and force your own point of view. Or you can listen, absorb, ask more, reflect, and accept/tweak/reject.
The first choice keeps you stagnant and makes you less likeable while the second helps you grow and even increases your likeability.
How to do it? Control the urge to respond - listen more, speak less. Focus - be present and absorb the information. Ask questions - be curious to know more. Think about it - take time, practice the pause. Exercise your agency - accept/reject/be indifferent.
Richard Feynman said, "I would rather have questions that can''t be answered than answers that can''t be questioned."', 37),
  ('Personal Growth & Mindset', '', 'Comparison Kills Joy', 'Last year in August, I went for the Kashmir Great Lakes trek. For anyone who knows a little about trekking would know that it is one of the most…', 'Last year in August, I went for the Kashmir Great Lakes trek. For anyone who knows a little about trekking would know that it is one of the most beautiful treks in India. But not for everyone.
You may find it hard to believe that a few people in a group of 20 were busy comparing it to some other treks they did in the past and weren''t charmed by the meadows and alpine lakes. For them, "The days were longer." "The landscape wasn''t changing much unlike their last one." "The food was better on that Uttarakhand trek."
The meadows and alpine lakes of Kashmir couldn''t make them happy. What killed the buzz? Comparison.
Try doing this to any place, person or an experience and you will always be disappointed. On the other hand, if you accept and enjoy the moment as is, small experiences will also bring a lot of joy.', 38),
  ('Personal Growth & Mindset', '', 'How to Love Your Own Company', 'As per Wikipedia, Loneliness is an unpleasant emotional response to perceived isolation. What is important to notice in these definitions are the…', 'As per Wikipedia, Loneliness is an unpleasant emotional response to perceived isolation. What is important to notice in these definitions are the phrases used - ''perceived isolation'' and ''feeling of being alone'' - which are not the same as ''being physically alone.'' And hence Loneliness is a state of mind and not a physical space.
And if it''s a state of mind, can you alter it? Yes, you can - by learning to enjoy your own company. And this can be done by doing something or nothing. Doing something makes sense but how to do nothing? Because doing nothing takes our mind to the career which is stagnant, finances which need realignment, health which needs improvement, relationships which need nurturing, or even worse to that colleague who got a bigger hike, that friend who recently bought a house, and that cousin who is taking a vacation in Maldives. And all these thoughts are uncomfortable - something which we intuitively avoid.
What''s the solution? The solution is to just observe our thoughts from a distance and let them pass - don''t accept or reject; just acknowledge and let them pass - breathe. And when we can do this, our loneliness turns into solitude - a place of comfort and enjoyment.
When you start enjoying your own company, you must be in good company.', 39),
  ('Personal Growth & Mindset', '', 'Physical Strength = Mental Strength', 'Physical strength is underrated and is as important as mental strength. Pick a room of 100 people. Ask if mental strength is more important or…', 'Physical strength is underrated and is as important as mental strength. Pick a room of 100 people. Ask if mental strength is more important or physical? More than 75 would vote for mental health.
This happens due to the following reasons: there is a high chance that a majority of those 75 people wouldn''t have built their physical strength by working out consistently, and hence don''t know how it feels to be physically strong; and the popular discourse of recent times is full of narratives about people who used their mental strength to achieve great things and these narratives help shape mindset.
But if you look after your body by eating well, sleeping well, and exercising regularly, the physical strength you build will keep you energized and feed your mental strength, the way your mental strength feeds your physical strength to help you complete those last 100 metres.', 40),
  ('Personal Growth & Mindset', '', 'Own Your Happiness', 'How do people around you look, when you are happy? Everyone looks good. And when you aren''t? Everyone seems to have some issue, especially the ones…', 'How do people around you look, when you are happy? Everyone looks good. And when you aren''t? Everyone seems to have some issue, especially the ones who are close - they generally bear the maximum brunt of it.
Often, it''s not about people around you being good or bad, it''s about how happy or unhappy you are with yourself which you project on others. A cloudy windy day looks romantic in good health while the same day feels like hell when you have a cold.
And being happy and peaceful everyday needs effort and accountability which we shy away from and keep attributing it to some external factor beyond our control.', 41),
  ('Personal Growth & Mindset', '', 'Maturity Is Moving From the Extremes to the Middle', 'I love cats, I hate dogs. I love tea, I hate coffee. I love mountains, I hate beaches. But why have such strong opinions about these trivial things?…', 'I love cats, I hate dogs. I love tea, I hate coffee. I love mountains, I hate beaches. But why have such strong opinions about these trivial things? You might prefer playing with or raising cats. You might prefer the taste of tea over coffee. You might prefer the weather in the mountains. This doesn''t make you love one and hate the other.
Words have the power to change the world and extreme words invoke extreme emotions. Unfortunately, this extremism has percolated all discourses on critical topics. You are either a Bhakt or Anti-Modi. You either have a six pack or a dad bod. You either work for 90 hours or just stare at the ceiling.
What happened to Augustine''s Perfect Moderation and Buddha''s Madhyam Marg? Maturity doesn''t come from grey hair, it comes from moving to grey from Black & White.', 42),
  ('Personal Growth & Mindset', '', 'Quick Wins Fade — Slow Success Lasts', 'Ever noticed how the high from a "big win" fades faster than you thought it would? Something you believed would make you very happy but when it…', 'Ever noticed how the high from a "big win" fades faster than you thought it would? Something you believed would make you very happy but when it finally happened, it felt fleeting, like chasing fireflies. That''s the problem with quick success; it feels great for a moment but soon leaves you empty, searching for the next one almost immediately.
Slow success, on the other hand, is different. It''s built on struggle, lessons, and persistence. It''s the kind of success that doesn''t just make you feel accomplished — it changes you as a person because you''ve earned it, step by step, through sweat and tears. It doesn''t get to your head and it keeps you humble.
And since joy needs to be created every single day, being on an upward journey - progressing slowly but consistently - brings far more lasting fulfilment than simply reaching the destination.', 43),
  ('Personal Growth & Mindset', '', 'Think!', 'Thinking hard about our lives helps. In 1952, Albert Schweitzer, a polymath and Nobel prize winner was asked: "What''s wrong with men today?" He…', 'Thinking hard about our lives helps. In 1952, Albert Schweitzer, a polymath and Nobel prize winner was asked: "What''s wrong with men today?" He replied: "Men simply don''t think."
Look around you and observe how many people think about their lives? And when I say ''think'', it''s not about what to watch on Netflix or how to plan their next vacation - it''s about sitting with themselves and thinking about the direction their lives are taking versus the direction which they want it to take.
Thinking about the direction of your life is a conscious act of reflection and course-correction and builds the foundation for everything else. The moment you start thinking about your Health, Relationships and Career & Wealth regularly, all of them start growing like magic.
Should thinking about life once a week not be the biggest resolution you should take? Sir Joshua Reynolds said, "There is no expedient to which a man will not resort, to avoid the real labour of thinking." Let''s not be those men in the new year and in the years to come!', 44),
  ('Personal Growth & Mindset', '', 'How Do You Want to Spend Your Life?', 'Your day consists of 8 hours of sleep, minimum 8 hours of work, and 8 hours for the rest of life. Out of the 24 hours in a day, work takes up at…', 'Your day consists of 8 hours of sleep, minimum 8 hours of work, and 8 hours for the rest of life. Out of the 24 hours in a day, work takes up at least a third. If you look at just your waking hours, half your day is spent working. Simply put, work takes up 33% of your life, and 50% of your conscious, awake life. If such is its magnitude, should it not be something which gives you joy?
Unfortunately, most people aren''t enjoying it but are doing their job to help them pay bills and leave enough to splurge on weekends or take a couple of luxurious vacations in a year - essentially compensating everyday fulfillment through intermittent dopamine surges.
If this is not a great way to live: be on a quest to find something which you enjoy doing (in most parts); be patient with time and don''t give up - it took me just 14 years. Once you figure out what it is, take deliberate steps which can take you closer to your goal - can''t be a full time author at this point? Start a blog or write on LinkedIn.
Don''t accept your current life as your fate; you can change it, slowly but surely. Start today!', 45),
  ('Personal Growth & Mindset', '', 'When Life Feels Stuck', '"Nothing seems to be going right in my life. What should I do?" It''s a question I hear often in coaching and counselling conversations. And it''s…', '"Nothing seems to be going right in my life. What should I do?" It''s a question I hear often in coaching and counselling conversations. And it''s usually followed by: I''m not feeling fit. My diet is all over the place. My relationships are strained. My career isn''t going anywhere. It feels like hitting rock bottom.
And how do I respond? I respond with a question: What is one small change you''re willing to commit to today? A 15 minute walk every morning? One nutritious meal a day? Practicing the pause - saying "Let me think about it" instead of reacting immediately? 10 minutes to plan your calendar every morning?
Because that 15 min walk will reconnect you with your health, which will nudge you toward better food choices, which will improve your sleep, which will boost your energy, which will bring back your focus. And focus will change everything.
And that''s why when you can''t stop yourself going down the rabbit-hole of negativity, your therapist asks you to start with ''cleaning your wardrobe.'' Because the simple act of doing something small and doable breaks the cycle of feeling stuck and snowballs into big momentum in all other areas of life.
So maybe today, you start small and let it lead to something big.', 46),
  ('Personal Growth & Mindset', '', 'Why You Can''t Stick to Healthy Habits (and How to Fix It)', 'Why is it so hard to eat right or sleep on time even when we know we should? I was speaking with a friend recently. He''s hypertensive. His doctor…', 'Why is it so hard to eat right or sleep on time even when we know we should? I was speaking with a friend recently. He''s hypertensive. His doctor warns him at every visit, but no real lifestyle change has happened.
I asked him, "Kuch changes kiye?" He said, "Nahi ho pata yaar." "Kyun?" "TV dekhte rehte hain raat ko, late ho jata hai... aur phir achha khaane ka mann karta hai to online order kar lete hain." "Toh jaldi so ja?" "2-4 din karta hoon, phir lagta hai... office ka pressure, ghar ka stress - thoda mazaa isi se aata hai. Wo bhi na lu?"
And there it was. May not be everyone''s story, but definitely a familiar one.
The brain craves pleasure. It avoids pain. And things like junk food and Netflix? They offer instant pleasure surge with zero effort. But why does our brain cling to these, even when they hurt us? Because it''s not getting joy from anywhere else.
And why is that? Because the right sources of joy - work that aligns with our strengths, relationships that help you feel connected, movement that energizes, stillness that brings clarity - don''t just show up. They need to be created, nurtured, and protected.
And what stops us from doing that? The long time and humongous effort it will take. Yet life itself is a long process and anything meaningful within it will take time too. We know what to do and today is a good day to start. It won''t be instant but it''ll be worth it.', 47),
  ('Personal Growth & Mindset', '', 'Religion Is a Pit; Spirituality Is a Deeper Pit', 'Let me tell you a secret: I am a believer. I believe in thinking. I believe in working. I believe in reflecting. I believe in introspecting. I…', 'Let me tell you a secret: I am a believer. I believe in thinking. I believe in working. I believe in reflecting. I believe in introspecting. I believe in knowing myself. I believe in yoga and meditation. And I believe in something which is beyond all this.
But that something isn''t Dogma. Guruism. Ritualism. Mysticism. Spirituality (as commodified). Cult personality. Spiritual bypassing. Enlightenment complex. And sacred but irrelevant texts.
What makes me think of this? Because the way people of my parents'' generation used to fall for Religion, the same way people of my generation are falling for Spirituality. And they are using it to escape everyday struggle of life, avoid dealing with unresolved emotional issues, and post cool stuff on social media.
For me, Spirituality is knowing yourself (physically, mentally and emotionally) and using that self-awareness to become a better version of yourself, every single day.', 48);
